{"@context":{"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","course":"identifier","wing":"isBasedOn","title":"name","principle":"abstract","skills":"keywords","lessons":"hasPart","key":"identifier","why":"description","statement":"mathExpression","address":"sameAs","skill":"keywords"},"@type":"Course","kind":"school-course","course":"Trinities","wing":"Trinities.lean","title":"Trinities","principle":"The trinities","skills":["trinities"],"level":100,"band":"100–999 steps","rank":115,"lessons":[{"key":"trinity_exceeds_qubit","name":"A TRINITY IS WORTH MORE THAN A QUBIT AND LESS THAN TWO: 2^1 < 3 < 2^2. Threes therefore never divide a binary space evenly, which is why a fold of fifteen leaves is five trinities and not a power of two.","statement":"((2:Nat)^1 < 3) ∧ (3 < (2:Nat)^2)","address":"7fcf294f-4f88-8336-80a8-1824a85a999e","skill":"trinities","tex":"2^{1} < 3 \\quad\\land\\quad 3 < 2^{2}","mathml":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mo>∧</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></math>","exercise":null},{"key":"trinities_span_powers","name":"A TRINITY IS THREE, and n of them span 3^n — the same shape as n qubits spanning 2^n. Walked from none to six: [1, 3, 9, 27, 81, 243, 729]. Nine is two trinities and eighty-one is four.","statement":"(List.range 7).map (fun n => 3^n) = [1,3,9,27,81,243,729]","address":"cd19de9c-29f0-8874-9fdc-73ef55162678","skill":"trinities","tex":null,"mathml":null,"exercise":{"kind":"predict-the-value","at":53,"length":3}},{"key":"eightyone_squares_nine","name":"THE COVERING NUMBER FOR THE ADDRESS SPACE IS THE RING'S OWN TABLE SIZE: 81 = 9^2 = 3^4, the full Z/9 multiplication table. SCOPE: this seals the arithmetic identity and nothing else. 128 divided by log2(3) is 80.76, so any space near 2^128 needs about eighty-one threes — the coincidence is decidable, its meaning is not, and no meaning is claimed here.","statement":"(81 = 9^2) ∧ (81 = 3^4) ∧ (9 = 3^2)","address":"12e3337d-3c1f-8847-ae51-c2684ef472fb","skill":"trinities","tex":"81 = 9^{2} \\quad\\land\\quad 81 = 3^{4} \\quad\\land\\quad 9 = 3^{2}","mathml":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>81</mn><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mn>9</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mo>∧</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mrow><mn>81</mn><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mo>∧</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></math>","exercise":{"kind":"predict-the-value","at":33,"length":1}},{"key":"trinities_cover_address","name":"COVERING 2^128 TAKES 81 TRINITIES, AND 80 DO NOT — both halves on one line, so this states THE bound and not merely a bound. 3^81 reaches it; 3^80 falls short. A theorem that only said \"81 suffice\" would be satisfied by any larger number and would seal nothing.","statement":"((3:Nat)^81 ≥ 2^128) ∧ ((3:Nat)^80 < 2^128)","address":"55734dfe-16fd-824f-83c2-fc4d995a5ac6","skill":"trinities","tex":"3^{81} \\ge 2^{128} \\quad\\land\\quad 3^{80} < 2^{128}","mathml":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>81</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≥</mo><msup><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>128</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mo>∧</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mrow><msup><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>80</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>128</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></math>","exercise":null},{"key":"trinities_cover_billion","name":"COVERING 10^9 TAKES 19 TRINITIES, AND 18 DO NOT — both halves on one line, so this states THE bound and not merely a bound. 3^19 reaches it; 3^18 falls short. A theorem that only said \"19 suffice\" would be satisfied by any larger number and would seal nothing.","statement":"((3:Nat)^19 ≥ 10^9) ∧ ((3:Nat)^18 < 10^9)","address":"4c3b2605-0cc3-8008-81d1-ec6fbd53e7ba","skill":"trinities","tex":"3^{19} \\ge 10^{9} \\quad\\land\\quad 3^{18} < 10^{9}","mathml":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>19</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≥</mo><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>9</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mo>∧</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mrow><msup><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>18</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>9</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></math>","exercise":null},{"key":"trinities_cover_word","name":"COVERING 2^64 TAKES 41 TRINITIES, AND 40 DO NOT — both halves on one line, so this states THE bound and not merely a bound. 3^41 reaches it; 3^40 falls short. A theorem that only said \"41 suffice\" would be satisfied by any larger number and would seal nothing.","statement":"((3:Nat)^41 ≥ 2^64) ∧ ((3:Nat)^40 < 2^64)","address":"911977cc-1d2b-821e-b765-b3e7c6452b8f","skill":"trinities","tex":"3^{41} \\ge 2^{64} \\quad\\land\\quad 3^{40} < 2^{64}","mathml":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>41</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≥</mo><msup><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>64</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mo>∧</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mrow><msup><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>40</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>64</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></math>","exercise":null}],"exercises":2}
